Dussehra 2026 falls on October 20, marking the end of Navratri and the symbolic victory of good over evil. It's a date most of us think about in terms of family, festivities, and a day off.

On the medical calendar, October 20 has quietly carried a few events worth knowing, not because they share a theme with the festival, but because four real, independently verifiable milestones happen to share this exact date. One is 410 years old. One is 214 years old. One is 42 years old. One is 9 years old.

## 1616: the anatomist who mapped a system nobody had seen whole

Thomas Bartholin, a Danish anatomist, was born on October 20, 1616. In December 1652, he published the first full description of the human lymphatic system, the network of vessels that quietly drains fluid, filters infection, and keeps the rest of circulation from backing up.

Before Bartholin, anatomists had seen pieces of this system without realizing the pieces belonged to one connected network. His description tied it together into the system still taught in first-year anatomy, and his family's name still shows up elsewhere in the body, including the Bartholin's glands in reproductive anatomy, work carried on by his son Caspar Bartholin the Younger.

{% callout type="tip" %}
Bartholin's birth date of October 20, 1616, is well documented by Britannica and Wikidata, though Denmark was still using the Julian calendar at the time. On a 400-year-old date, treat the exact day as the best available record rather than an exact certainty.
{% /callout %}

## 1812: the murmur that still gets tested on clinical exams

Austin Flint was born in Petersham, Massachusetts, on October 20, 1812. He became one of the most prominent American physicians of his century, helped found Buffalo Medical College, and served as president of the American Medical Association from 1884 to 1885.

His name survives in cardiology for a specific finding: the Austin Flint murmur, a soft, rumbling sound heard at the heart's apex in patients with significant aortic regurgitation, caused by the leaking valve's jet interfering with normal flow through the mitral valve. More than 160 years after Flint first described it, it's still a standard finding taught for clinical exams and still something a stethoscope can pick up at the bedside.

## 1984: the quiet end of a cycle that's still on your biochemistry syllabus

Carl Ferdinand Cori died on October 20, 1984, in Cambridge, Massachusetts, according to the Nobel Prize's own biographical record. With his wife and research partner, Gerty Cori, he had shared the 1947 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Bernardo Houssay, for working out how the body converts glycogen into glucose and back again, the pathway every MBBS student still learns as the Cori cycle.

It's not a flashy discovery by modern standards, no headline, no press conference. It's just the biochemistry behind how your muscles get energy during exercise and how your liver restores blood sugar afterward, worked out by a husband-and-wife lab in an era when that itself was unusual. Gerty Cori was the first American woman to win a Nobel Prize in a scientific field.

{% callout type="summary" %}
The Cori cycle isn't an old idea sitting in a textbook footnote. It's the reason your biochemistry exam still asks how lactate from your muscles turns back into glucose in your liver.
{% /callout %}

## 2017: a vaccine that changed how shingles gets prevented

On October 20, 2017, the FDA approved Shingrix, a non-live recombinant vaccine for preventing shingles in adults 50 and older. The approval letter is dated that day, and the vaccine's clinical trials had shown more than 90% efficacy across age groups, a meaningfully higher bar than the live vaccine it eventually replaced as the preferred option.

{% statCallout
   number="90%+"
   label="Efficacy shown across age groups in the clinical trials behind Shingrix, the shingles vaccine the FDA approved on October 20, 2017." /%}

Shingles isn't exotic. It's the same varicella-zoster virus that causes chickenpox, reactivating later in life, and it gets more common and more painful with age. A vaccine with efficacy this high, for a condition this common, is the kind of approval that doesn't trend on social media but changes outcomes for millions of people over the following years.

{% callout type="tip" %}
Also observed every October 20: World Osteoporosis Day, run by the International Osteoporosis Foundation since 1997. It's an awareness campaign, not a discovery, but it's a reminder that bone health gets far less attention than it deserves until a fracture forces the issue.
{% /callout %}

## Four dates, one honest thread

A 17th-century anatomist who mapped a system nobody had seen whole. A 19th-century physician whose murmur is still a clinical exam answer. A Nobel-winning biochemistry discovery whose namesake died on this date. A vaccine approval that quietly reduced a common, painful illness. None of these were planned to coincide. They just did, the way a lot of real history turns out to be coincidence dressed up afterward as pattern.

What connects them isn't the date. It's that each one holds up when you check it: Bartholin's lymphatic system is in the same anatomy atlas you're using right now, the Austin Flint murmur is still asked about in cardiology vivas, the Cori cycle is still in your syllabus because it's still correct, and Shingrix's efficacy numbers are in the FDA's own approval record, not a headline summarizing a headline.

## Why checking the date mattered here

Writing this meant throwing out more claims than we kept. One widely repeated "on this day" list credited October 20 with the first successful heart transplant and the completion of the Human Genome Project. Neither happened on this date, the transplant was in December 1967, the genome project wrapped in April 2003. A different source placed a drug's approval in October when the real date was September. Those errors spread because nobody went back to the primary record.

{% actionBox title="Build that habit here first" %}
It's easy to repeat a fact because it sounds right, especially in medicine, where confident-sounding claims travel fast and get fact-checked slowly, if at all. A wrong exam answer is recoverable. A wrong clinical assumption, carried for years because nobody rechecked it, usually isn't.

